{
  "passage": "Flowers are the reproductive parts of a plant. The male part of a flower is the stamen, which produces pollen grains. The female part is the pistil, which contains the ovules in its lower part, the ovary. For seeds to form, pollen must reach the sticky top of the pistil, called the stigma. The transfer of pollen from the stamen to the stigma is called pollination. If the pollen lands on the stigma of the same flower, it is called self-pollination. If it is carried to a flower on another plant of the same kind, it is called cross-pollination. Plants cannot move, so they depend on carriers. Wind carries the light, dry pollen of maize and grasses over long distances. Insects such as bees and butterflies visit brightly coloured, sweet-smelling flowers to drink nectar. As they move from flower to flower, pollen sticks to their bodies and is carried along. After pollination, the pollen grain grows a tube down to the ovule, and the male cell joins the egg cell. This joining is called fertilisation. The fertilised egg develops into an embryo, the ovule becomes a seed, and the ovary grows into a fruit. Seeds are then scattered away from the parent plant, by wind, water and animals, so that the new plants do not compete with each other for light and water.",
